From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Offload <offload@localhost>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:21:19 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(payload): add login sessions without rewriting the
 sessions array

Concurrent logins as one user fail or lose their sessions. Login reads the
user with its sessions, hashes the password, then writes the whole user back
with the new session appended. The SQL adapters write an array by deleting
all of its rows and inserting them again. D1 has no transactions, so the
logins interleave those statements: eight logins at once return one 200 and
seven 500s with "UNIQUE constraint failed: users_sessions.id". On Postgres
every login returns 200, but each one writes back the sessions it read, so
the sessions of the other logins disappear and their tokens stop working.
Logging out of one session, and the cleanup after a login fails, rewrite the
array the same way.

Login now removes the expired sessions it read, by ID, then adds its session
with $push, which the SQL adapters write as one insert. Logging out of one
session and the cleanup after a failed login remove only that session.
updateOne accepts { $remove: id | id[] } on a top-level array field: the SQL
adapters delete those rows, in batches that stay within D1's 100 bound
parameters, and MongoDB pulls them. Session expiry, logout from all sessions,
refresh and password changes keep their behaviour. MongoDB with transactions
still rejects concurrent writes to one user with a WriteConflict, as before.

Offload-Patch: concurrent-login-sessions
Upstream: https://github.com/payloadcms/payload/issues/16027
Drop-When: the pinned release adds a login session without writing back the sessions array it read (issue 16027, PR 18150)
Tests: pnpm exec vitest run --project unit packages/drizzle/src/upsertRow/removeArrayRows.spec.ts; PAYLOAD_DATABASE=sqlite pnpm test:int test/auth/int.spec.ts; e2e suite (concurrent logins test)
